\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A stable focus of FitzHugh–Nagumo}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take the FitzHugh–Nagumo velocity of \ref{fnip-0018} with
#{I=0}, #{a=0.1}, #{b=0.01}, and #{c=0.02}. The origin is the
equilibrium. The first component is #{V(a-V)(V-1)-w+I}. Its
derivative in #{V} equals #{-a} at the origin, because
##{V(a-V)(V-1)=-V^{3}+(a+1)V^{2}-aV}
and the derivative of that polynomial at #{0} is #{-a}. The
derivative in #{w} is #{-1}. The second component #{bV-cw} has
derivatives #{b} and #{-c}. The [Jacobian](fnip-0034) at the origin
is therefore
##{Df(0,0)=\begin{pmatrix}-a&-1\\b&-c\end{pmatrix}.}
\citet{p. 107}{izhikevich2007dynamical} prints this matrix as
#{L}, with trace #{\tau=-a-c} and determinant #{\Delta=ac+b}.
For these parameter values #{\tau<0}, #{\Delta>0}, and
#{\tau^{2}-4\Delta<0}. The eigenvalues are a complex conjugate
pair with negative real part, so the origin is a stable
[focus](fnip-0035).}
